      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find two integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) whose least common multiple (LCM) is 36 and whose greatest common divisor (GCD) is 6. Let's denote the two numbers as \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n\nFirst, recall the relationship between the LCM and GCD of two numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) \\times \\text{GCD}(a, b) = a \\times b\n\\]\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(a, b) = 36 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\n\\]\n\nUsing the relationship, we can substitute the given values:\n\\[\n36 \\times 6 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\\[\n216 = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to express \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) in terms of their GCD. If \\(\\text{GCD}(a, b) = 6\\), then we can write:\n\\[\na = 6m \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 6n\n\\]\nwhere \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are coprime integers (i.e., \\(\\text{GCD}(m, n) = 1\\)).\n\nSubstituting these into the product equation \\(a \\times b = 216\\), we get:\n\\[\n(6m) \\times (6n) = 216\n\\]\n\\[\n36mn = 216\n\\]\n\\[\nmn = \\frac{216}{36}\n\\]\n\\[\nmn = 6\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find coprime pairs \\((m, n)\\) such that \\(mn = 6\\). The pairs \\((m, n)\\) are:\n\\[\n(1, 6), (2, 3), (3, 2), (6, 1)\n\\]\n\nAll these pairs are coprime, so we can use any of them. Let's choose \\(m = 2\\) and \\(n = 3\\) (or any other pair, but this will work for our calculation).\n\nThus:\n\\[\na = 6m = 6 \\times 2 = 12\n\\]\n\\[\nb = 6n = 6 \\times 3 = 18\n\\]\n\nFinally, the product of the two numbers is:\n\\[\na \\times b = 12 \\times 18\n\\]\n\nTo find this product:\n\\[\n12 \\times 18 = 12 \\times (20 - 2) = 12 \\times 20 - 12 \\times 2 = 240 - 24 = 216\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{216}\n\\]",
